So buy it.

00:24:47.830 --> 00:24:51.847
Here's another question
from @scienceisanart.

00:24:53.499 --> 00:24:54.332
Huh.

00:24:55.470 --> 00:24:58.120
I moved in with my
boyfriend a few months ago

00:24:58.120 --> 00:25:00.240
and he has apparently never washed a dish

00:25:00.240 --> 00:25:03.660
or cooked anything more
complicated than ramen in his life.

00:25:03.660 --> 00:25:05.260
Okay.
Apparently spaghetti

00:25:05.260 --> 00:25:08.620
is too complex for him
so what most basic dish

00:25:08.620 --> 00:25:12.560
can I teach him to introduce into cooking?

00:25:12.560 --> 00:25:14.470
Let me say that last part in
a way that makes more sense.

00:25:14.470 --> 00:25:16.670
So what basic dish can I teach him

00:25:16.670 --> 00:25:18.070
to introduce him to cooking?

00:25:19.850 --> 00:25:20.683
Okay.

00:25:21.560 --> 00:25:25.116
Well you know for me, I'm not a cooker.

00:25:25.116 --> 00:25:28.077
I'm not a cheffer, I'm
not a maker of dishes.

00:25:30.272 --> 00:25:35.272
I'm not proud of that
but it's just a fact.

00:25:35.914 --> 00:25:40.914
Nothing about learning how to
cook has ever appealed to me.

00:25:41.360 --> 00:25:44.940
And again, it's almost a confession

00:25:44.940 --> 00:25:46.240
but you already know that.

00:25:48.170 --> 00:25:50.220
I am however a cleaner.

00:25:50.220 --> 00:25:51.870
So I'm observing right off the bat--

00:25:51.870 --> 00:25:55.100
So you like the part that no one does.

00:25:55.100 --> 00:25:58.280
You pick the only thing
that, part of the process

00:25:58.280 --> 00:25:59.130
that nobody likes.

00:25:59.130 --> 00:26:02.142
Well the way that I've
always justified it,

00:26:02.142 --> 00:26:07.142
and Christy enjoys cooking
sometimes, not all the time,

00:26:07.210 --> 00:26:09.039
but because I don't enjoy cooking at all,

00:26:09.039 --> 00:26:10.900
if someone's gonna cook,

00:26:10.900 --> 00:26:13.620
it's on her until now the
kids have gotten old enough

00:26:13.620 --> 00:26:15.040
to start preparing things themselves,

00:26:15.040 --> 00:26:18.350
especially Lily who also
enjoys cooking and baking

00:26:19.350 --> 00:26:22.343
to a large extent.

00:26:25.242 --> 00:26:30.242
So I've always felt guilty
that she's been expected

00:26:30.940 --> 00:26:35.940
to do the cooking and
not in like a masculine,

00:26:36.772 --> 00:26:39.080
feminine role type way but just

00:26:39.080 --> 00:26:40.530
in a personal taste kinda way.

00:26:40.530 --> 00:26:42.640
Honestly I believe that's why.

00:26:42.640 --> 00:26:44.640
Not because she's the woman or anything.

00:26:45.880 --> 00:26:48.410
But I felt bad about that but
I've always compensated her

00:26:48.410 --> 00:26:51.180
taking a defensive stance
of, well at least I'll clean.

00:26:51.180 --> 00:26:54.970
Like hey, I made a decision
early on in our marriage

00:26:54.970 --> 00:26:57.370
because I didn't know how to
cook and for whatever reasons

00:26:57.370 --> 00:27:00.450
which I can go into if you want me to.

00:27:00.450 --> 00:27:03.020
I've said, I'm just
gonna take the stance of,

00:27:03.020 --> 00:27:06.400
I'm gonna be grateful and
uncritical of anything

00:27:06.400 --> 00:27:09.050
that you wanna prepare because
I know that I would die

00:27:09.050 --> 00:27:14.050
if you didn't do it, and
I will also then clean up.

00:27:14.790 --> 00:27:18.500
So I think I got a good track record.

00:27:18.500 --> 00:27:19.630
I'm not gonna say a great one

00:27:19.630 --> 00:27:21.563
because my wife's probably listening.

00:27:22.420 --> 00:27:25.290
But at least a decent track
record of at least compensating

00:27:25.290 --> 00:27:26.640
with not cooking by cleaning.

00:27:26.640 --> 00:27:27.850
You also like cleaning.

00:27:27.850 --> 00:27:29.480
And I like cleaning.

00:27:29.480 --> 00:27:32.020
I like putting things
away and a lot of people

00:27:32.020 --> 00:27:34.970
who enjoy cooking, they start to say,

00:27:34.970 --> 00:27:37.546
and Christy has said in the past,

00:27:37.546 --> 00:27:40.210
and I've heard chefs as
I've spoken with many chefs,

00:27:40.210 --> 00:27:41.750
it's like, the only part I hate

00:27:41.750 --> 00:27:42.990
about cooking is the cleaning.

00:27:42.990 --> 00:27:45.278
It's like well let me take care of that.

00:27:45.278 --> 00:27:48.893
As an offering of gratitude.

00:27:49.870 --> 00:27:53.490
But this guy does neither
and I find it interesting

00:27:53.490 --> 00:27:55.890
that the thing that she's asking about is,

00:27:55.890 --> 00:27:57.654
she's keying in on the cooking part

00:27:57.654 --> 00:28:00.230
and maybe that's 'cause
she likes the cleaning part

00:28:00.230 --> 00:28:02.810
so I respect that but now I'm a little out

00:28:02.810 --> 00:28:04.950
of my league because--
Well let me ask you this.

00:28:04.950 --> 00:28:07.783
So I'm just telling, buddy,
he should do one or the other.

00:28:07.783 --> 00:28:10.913
Well I think maybe you
could speak to this.

00:28:10.913 --> 00:28:14.060
So first of all, it is
interesting that you don't cook,

00:28:14.060 --> 00:28:17.670
right, I think if you were
to analyze not personality,

00:28:17.670 --> 00:28:19.673
but if you were to analyze background,

00:28:21.300 --> 00:28:23.568
I was raised in a very traditional home

00:28:23.568 --> 00:28:28.160
where my mom did all the household duties.

00:28:28.160 --> 00:28:31.710
My dad couldn't cook anything,
wouldn't cook anything.

00:28:31.710 --> 00:28:33.320
He basically--
He'd grill a steak.

00:28:33.320 --> 00:28:35.240
Yeah he was the grill
man, he was the grill man.

00:28:35.240 --> 00:28:37.320
He'll grill a steak, he'll
barbecue the chicken,

00:28:37.320 --> 00:28:39.920
but it's basically, I
don't know what it is

00:28:40.790 --> 00:28:42.790
about baby boomer men but it's like

00:28:42.790 --> 00:28:44.941
I'm only gonna cook if I'm outside.

00:28:44.941 --> 00:28:47.440
The moment that I come in it's like

00:28:47.440 --> 00:28:49.190
this isn't my territory.

00:28:49.190 --> 00:28:50.440
Lots of reasons for that.

00:28:51.440 --> 00:28:54.960
And so, my mom did everything for us.

00:28:54.960 --> 00:28:58.362
Like, my brother and I never did laundry.

00:28:58.362 --> 00:29:00.840
I think she was just like, you guys,

00:29:00.840 --> 00:29:04.520
she didn't have the patience
for us to make the mistakes

00:29:04.520 --> 00:29:05.920
that we would have made.

00:29:05.920 --> 00:29:08.100
She was just like, "Just
let me do that," you know,

00:29:08.100 --> 00:29:10.060
and that's kind of a baby
boomer mom kind of thing

00:29:10.060 --> 00:29:11.293
in a lot of ways.

00:29:12.530 --> 00:29:16.670
So going into college,
I never cooked anything.

00:29:16.670 --> 00:29:20.880
But I liked to eat so much

00:29:20.880 --> 00:29:23.230
that there came a time
in which I was like,

00:29:23.230 --> 00:29:24.940
I have to cook for myself.

00:29:24.940 --> 00:29:28.930
It wasn't like, I wanna be a cooker.

00:29:28.930 --> 00:29:32.620
You know, it was like I
want to cook so I can eat.

00:29:32.620 --> 00:29:35.913
And I love it and--
So in our dorm room,

00:29:36.941 --> 00:29:41.266
we had one of those toaster oven things

00:29:41.266 --> 00:29:43.310
and I don't recall using that much.

00:29:43.310 --> 00:29:45.333
Not really cooking, even
if I were to use it but--

00:29:45.333 --> 00:29:46.570
So then you're referring to

00:29:46.570 --> 00:29:48.540
when we got our first
apartment that had a kitchen.

00:29:48.540 --> 00:29:50.330
Yeah and--
You started cooking stuff.

00:29:50.330 --> 00:29:54.200
Yeah and also, Greg
could cook a little bit.

00:29:54.200 --> 00:29:55.470
Tim could cook a little bit.

00:29:55.470 --> 00:29:57.470
We all kinda could cook a
little bit but it was like,

00:29:57.470 --> 00:29:59.410
okay I'm gonna make Hamburger Helper.

00:29:59.410 --> 00:30:01.920
I'm gonna make mashed
potatoes out of a box.

00:30:01.920 --> 00:30:04.100
But you never, you were like I'll eat it

00:30:04.100 --> 00:30:04.933
but you never were like,

00:30:04.933 --> 00:30:06.230
I'm gonna make the Hamburger Helper.

00:30:06.230 --> 00:30:08.059
I'm gonna fry up the hamburger.

00:30:08.059 --> 00:30:09.430
Well you know what,
Rhett there's a saying.

00:30:09.430 --> 00:30:11.640
It goes a little something like this.

00:30:11.640 --> 00:30:15.234
Too many cooks in the kitchen.

00:30:15.234 --> 00:30:16.700
I mean there's four guys
livin' in an apartment,

00:30:16.700 --> 00:30:18.163
three of 'em are cooking in the kitchen.

00:30:18.163 --> 00:30:20.240
Well no but here's the thing, we would--

00:30:20.240 --> 00:30:21.830
There was also the laundry
was in the kitchen.

00:30:21.830 --> 00:30:23.500
We would divide up the cooking.

00:30:23.500 --> 00:30:25.300
It would be like tonight
Greg is gonna make this

00:30:25.300 --> 00:30:26.650
or tonight Tim is gonna make it,

00:30:26.650 --> 00:30:27.910
tonight Rhett is gonna make it

00:30:27.910 --> 00:30:29.650
but there was never a tonight
Link is gonna make it.

00:30:29.650 --> 00:30:31.750
That never worked into the mix.

00:30:31.750 --> 00:30:32.990
But I find it interesting.

00:30:32.990 --> 00:30:34.500
We never talked about it, I didn't care.

00:30:34.500 --> 00:30:36.233
I have no ill will about it.

00:30:37.084 --> 00:30:39.230
But you were actually on your own a lot.

00:30:39.230 --> 00:30:40.260
Yeah.
Greg being raised

00:30:40.260 --> 00:30:42.980
by a single mom and
being home alone a lot,

00:30:42.980 --> 00:30:45.590
like you could have
cooked a lot of things.

00:30:45.590 --> 00:30:47.010
You know what--
So why didn't you?

00:30:47.010 --> 00:30:48.680
I think maybe--
'Cause you gotta get

00:30:48.680 --> 00:30:50.620
into this guy's head.
I think that plays into it

00:30:50.620 --> 00:30:55.620
because I think my mom,
because she had so many demands

00:30:55.630 --> 00:31:00.630
on her life and feeding
me being just one of 'em,

00:31:02.440 --> 00:31:06.310
I think it was like,
anything that was pre-made

00:31:06.310 --> 00:31:08.010
at the grocery store we needed to get.

00:31:08.010 --> 00:31:12.280
We needed, she didn't have
the time or the energy

00:31:13.400 --> 00:31:16.110
to cook as much so let's
work with that theory

00:31:16.110 --> 00:31:18.710
because I, fact.

00:31:18.710 --> 00:31:21.070
Everything in our house was like,

00:31:21.070 --> 00:31:23.780
you pull up the foil top
and then you can just,

00:31:23.780 --> 00:31:25.500
you eat it with a spoon.

00:31:25.500 --> 00:31:28.260
Applesauce, pudding.
All the best things.

00:31:28.260 --> 00:31:29.400
All the best things.

00:31:29.400 --> 00:31:32.030
And then bars, you can
just open up and eat.

00:31:32.030 --> 00:31:33.720
Or cookies.
But you didn't eat bars

00:31:33.720 --> 00:31:34.570
for dinner.

00:31:34.570 --> 00:31:36.527
I mean she always cooked when I came over.

00:31:36.527 --> 00:31:37.420
She wouldn't--

00:31:37.420 --> 00:31:39.276
She had that stove top--

00:31:39.276 --> 00:31:40.873
Skillet.
Skillet thing

00:31:40.873 --> 00:31:42.690
that you put on the counter, yeah.

00:31:42.690 --> 00:31:46.130
Yeah she had her, we talked about

00:31:46.130 --> 00:31:51.090
in the Book of Mythicality,
she had her few meals

00:31:51.090 --> 00:31:54.030
that she would, she'd serve--
Rotation, yeah.

00:31:54.030 --> 00:31:55.703
She had the rotation.

00:31:56.820 --> 00:31:59.470
I don't know, maybe that has
something to do with it though

00:31:59.470 --> 00:32:02.380
is that a lot of it was stilL packaged

00:32:02.380 --> 00:32:05.280
and I was, I would be
home alone for lunches

00:32:05.280 --> 00:32:10.280
over the summer and I remember
my favorite thing was,

00:32:10.710 --> 00:32:13.210
you'd peel the top off of this package

00:32:13.210 --> 00:32:15.970
that would have roasted chicken legs

00:32:15.970 --> 00:32:18.683
from like Holly Farms or
whatever it was called.

00:32:19.990 --> 00:32:22.620
Roasted, fully cooked chicken legs

00:32:22.620 --> 00:32:25.570
and I wouldn't even eat 'em
hot, I would just eat 'em cold.

00:32:26.780 --> 00:32:28.890
It's like so easy.

00:32:28.890 --> 00:32:30.530
Right.
So it's like it's so easy,

00:32:30.530 --> 00:32:31.693
it's so tasty.

00:32:32.550 --> 00:32:33.660
I was rewarded.

00:32:33.660 --> 00:32:37.166
Why go through effort when
I gotta do is rip the top

00:32:37.166 --> 00:32:38.323
off of this thing?

00:32:39.420 --> 00:32:41.770
That's what I learned as a child.

00:32:41.770 --> 00:32:43.800
Just rip the top off, brotha.

00:32:43.800 --> 00:32:44.900
Get to something else.

00:32:46.040 --> 00:32:49.589
Like playing cards by
yourself in your room,

00:32:49.589 --> 00:32:52.920
looking at your G.I. Joes.
Yeah I mean, to me,

00:32:52.920 --> 00:32:56.163
so I think that when
you think about cooking,

00:32:58.117 --> 00:33:01.219
there is no joy, the joy of cooking.

00:33:01.219 --> 00:33:02.860
I've never experienced it.

00:33:02.860 --> 00:33:06.630
Ironically, I mean, when
I took that cooking class

00:33:06.630 --> 00:33:09.410
in Thailand, I started, I was like oh,

00:33:09.410 --> 00:33:11.857
I had this knife for a
second, I was chopping stuff

00:33:11.857 --> 00:33:15.640
and I actually, he made the
meal and then we made it.

00:33:15.640 --> 00:33:19.380
It's like I could see
how this could be fun

00:33:19.380 --> 00:33:22.120
but it felt like an entirely
new experience to me,

00:33:22.120 --> 00:33:24.380
like I never experienced the fact

00:33:24.380 --> 00:33:28.030
of putting stuff together and also,

00:33:28.030 --> 00:33:30.540
I think there's anxiety
of having never done it,

00:33:30.540 --> 00:33:33.040
it's like all I'm gonna
do is screw this up.

00:33:33.040 --> 00:33:34.220
There's no way this is gonna work.

00:33:34.220 --> 00:33:38.720
Fear of failure.
There's a lot of steps,

00:33:38.720 --> 00:33:40.930
there's a lot of things to get
right and if I'm gonna do it,

00:33:40.930 --> 00:33:42.240
I gotta do it exactly right.
I think maybe--

00:33:42.240 --> 00:33:44.379
So it's a lot of pressure.
